{
	title: 'Tab indicators that blink like the old Hal',
	spec: "The tab bar's per-tab indicator follows the old Hal's tabIndicator (artifact old-render-status.ts in task 5p): a question '!' (steady, warning colour); working with new attention '◆' (blinking, warning colour); working '▪' (blinking, Hal cursor colour); failed and retrying '✗' (blinking, error colour); paused, waiting for continue '!' (steady); new attention '◆' (steady); finished and not yet seen '✓' (steady, done colour). Blinking uses the shared pulse of task 5p; where the lit and dim colours would look the same (monochrome terminal) the glyph blinks by being there or not. The mapping lives in one common module (src/common/tab-mark.ts) that both the terminal tab bar and the web tab strip use, so a tab shows the same mark in every client (the web once showed '?' where the terminal showed '!'); each client only colours it by kind, and the web pulses the blinking ones. Map Hal's session states (tasks/j1/states.md) onto these, not old Hal fields. Tests: the indicator for each state, and that blinking indicators alternate while steady ones do not.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Mapping (client/tab-bar.ts indicator): blocked (question or login) '!' steady warning; running with attention '◆' blinking warning; running '▪' blinking assistant cursor colour (dark phase cursorIdle); retrying and error '✗' blinking error colour; paused '!' steady pausedFg; idle with attention '✓' steady doneFg. Hal's attention (host/tabs.ts) is set when a turn ends, fails or asks, so on an idle tab it means finished and unseen; there is no separate 'new' attention, so the old steady '◆' has no state of its own.",
		"Error blinks like retrying: the old Hal's 'retry' continue action (✗ blinking) was the failed turn waiting for the user, which is Hal's error state.",
		'Blink plumbing: app.view sets view.tabs.lit = pulse.slow(beat) only when tabBar.blinks(list); app.blinks keys on [hal, lit], so the pulse runs for a blinking tab even with no transcript. Other dark colours are the lit one at 0.65 lightness; when ansi.sgr gives the same escape for both (ansi.mono) the glyph becomes a space of the same width, so the bar never shifts.'
